.TH EVENT_LOOP_PAUSE 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_pause \- temporarily stop delivering events for an fd registration, without destroying it
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t event_loop_pause(event_loop loop, event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
fd-only: called on a registration built from a queue/channel selectable,
returns ccol_invalid_args, same restriction as event_loop_modify.

Unlike event_loop_remove (which fully unregisters reg and defers it
for freeing), event_loop_pause leaves reg fully intact: it still
occupies its slot on the underlying fd's shared epoll entry, still
counts toward event_loop_reg_count, and still carries the same
event_loop_reg_generation.  The fd's combined epoll interest mask is
recomputed to exclude reg; if the other direction on the same fd (if
any) is itself live and unpaused, this is a single EPOLL_CTL_MOD call,
otherwise (every direction on the fd is now paused) the fd is removed
from the kernel's own epoll interest set entirely, so it produces zero
further wakeups of any kind for as long as it stays fully paused,
including on its own error/hangup condition (which the kernel would
otherwise keep reporting regardless of the requested interest mask).
No on_readable/on_writable/on_error callback fires for reg while
paused; the other direction on the same fd (if any) is unaffected.

This is the cheap alternative to an event_loop_remove immediately
followed by a later event_loop_add, for a caller pattern where the
same logical registration is going to come back; for example a
connection handed off to a worker thread for blocking body I/O, then
handed back to the reactor for its next request.  Avoided entirely:
the heap allocation/free a fresh event_loop_add/event_loop_remove pair
costs, and the fd-registry chmap churn that comes with it.

With more than one reactor thread (num_reactor_threads > 1, where
EPOLLONESHOT is in play), a dispatch worker's own post-callback
re-arm still runs once more after a paused callback returns.  This is
harmless: event_loop's internal re-arm helper always recomputes the
epoll mask fresh from live state, so the redundant re-arm reapplies
the same excluding mask rather than reintroducing a race.  Pause/
resume still avoids the allocation and registry churn in that
configuration too.

Pausing an already-paused reg is a no-op success.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to pause.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        Paused (or already paused).
ccol_invalid_args   loop is EVENT_LOOP_INVALID or a stale/already-
                    destroyed handle; reg is EVENT_REG_INVALID or
                    otherwise fails to resolve (including because it
                    was concurrently removed); or reg is a queue/
                    channel registration.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call concurrently with event_loop_remove() and from within a
callback running on one of the reactor threads.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Inside an on_readable callback, once a full request's headers have
   been parsed and the connection is about to be handed to a worker
   thread for its own direct, blocking body I/O: */
event_loop_pause(loop, conn->reg);
ctpool_submit(worker_pool, process_request, conn, NULL);
/* ... later, on the worker thread, once the response has been sent
   and the connection is ready for its next request: */
event_loop_resume(loop, conn->reg);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_resume (3),
.BR event_loop_add (3),
.BR event_loop_remove (3),
.BR event_loop_modify (3),
.BR event_loop_reg_generation (3)
